.TH CYAML 7 "2026-10-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
cyaml \- overview of the YAML 1.2 parser, serializer and DOM
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
cyaml parses YAML text into a tree of nodes.  A program can read and
change that tree.  cyaml then writes the tree back as YAML.  Each node
has an opaque cyaml handle.  cyaml_parse(3) describes the input that the
parser accepts, and the limits that it applies.  This page gives the
rules of the YAML language that are not specific to one function.
.fi
.SH "NODE TYPES"
.nf
cyaml_type(3) gives the type of a node, as a cyaml_node_type_t:

  CYAML_NULL         null, ~, or an empty value
  CYAML_BOOL         true or false, read with cyaml_bool_val(3)
  CYAML_INTEGER      a long long, read with cyaml_int_val(3)
  CYAML_FLOAT        a double, read with cyaml_double_val(3)
  CYAML_STRING       a UTF-8 string, read with cyaml_str_val(3)
  CYAML_LIST         a sequence of child nodes
  CYAML_DICTIONARY   a mapping from a string key to a child node

The keys of a dictionary are plain strings, and not nodes.  The
document can write a key as a number, a boolean, null or a collection.
The dictionary then keeps that key as canonical text.  See
cyaml_parse(3).
.fi
.SH "IMPLICIT TYPING"
.nf
The parser gives a type to a plain (unquoted, untagged) scalar with the
YAML 1.2 core schema:

  ~, null, Null, NULL, or empty          CYAML_NULL
  true, True, TRUE                       CYAML_BOOL (true)
  false, False, FALSE                    CYAML_BOOL (false)
  decimal integer, 0x hex, 0o octal      CYAML_INTEGER
  decimal float, exponent notation       CYAML_FLOAT
  .inf, -.inf, .nan (any case)           CYAML_FLOAT
  anything else                          CYAML_STRING

Under YAML 1.2, yes, no, on and off are strings.  An integer literal
can be outside the range of a long long.  It then becomes a CYAML_FLOAT.
If it is not a float either, it becomes a CYAML_STRING.  It never wraps.
A single-quoted or double-quoted scalar is always a string, whatever
its text.  A tag can override all these rules.  See TAGS.
.fi
.SH "TAGS"
.nf
A tag attaches to its node.  A tag can be !!str, a custom !foo, a %TAG
shorthand, or a verbatim !<...>.  cyaml_node_tag(3) gives the tag back
in fully resolved form.  There are five scalar core-schema tags:
!!null, !!bool, !!int, !!float and !!str.  Each one forces its type on
a scalar of any style.  This type replaces the type that the text alone
gives.  For example, "!!str 42" is the string "42".  !!bool, !!int and
!!float examine the text of their scalar.  If the text does not match,
the parse fails.  !!null accepts any text.  A custom tag forces no type.
The node keeps its implicit type.

A tag on a dictionary key gives a type to the key in the same way.  The
dictionary keeps the canonical text of the typed key.  It does not keep
the tag.  The serializers write a custom tag in its verbatim !<...>
form, and never in a %TAG shorthand.  They do not write a core-schema
tag, because implicit typing gives the same type again.  See
cyaml_serialize(3).  cyaml_node_set_tag(3) and cyaml_set(3) keep a
core-schema tag only while it names the type of its node.
.fi
.SH "SUPPORTED YAML"
.nf
Block mappings, with implicit keys ("key: value") and explicit keys
("? key" then ": value").  One mapping can contain the two kinds of
keys in any sequence.  The ':' of an explicit value has this function
only when a space, a tab, a line end or the end of input comes after
it.  Therefore, ":name: value" is an ordinary entry, and its key is ":name".
An implicit key is on one line.  An explicit key can continue on
several lines.

Block sequences ("- item").  A sequence can be the value of a mapping
key.  Such a sequence can have the same indentation as that key.

Flow mappings ("{a: 1, b: 2}").  A bare key with no ':' gives a null
value.  A ':' with no value also gives a null value.  The parser
accepts an explicit "? key: value" entry.  Its key can continue on
several lines.

Flow sequences ("[a, b, c]").  A "key: value" pair in a flow sequence
is a mapping with one entry.  Therefore, "[foo: bar]" is equal to
"[{foo: bar}]".  The parser accepts an explicit "[? key: value]" entry.

Plain, single-quoted and double-quoted scalars.  Each of them can
continue on several lines.  A line break becomes a space.  A blank line
becomes a newline.  A single-quoted scalar has no escapes.  In it, ''
is one quote.  A double-quoted scalar accepts all YAML escapes, and
\euXXXX too.

Literal (|) and folded (>) block scalars, with the chomping indicators
|+ (keep), |- (strip) and | (clip, the default).

Anchors, aliases, tags and merge keys.  See below.

The %YAML directive.  It can occur at most one time in each document.
The parser examines it against the MAJOR.MINOR grammar.

The %TAG directive.  Its prefixes apply to the document that declares
them.

Documents with "---" and "..." markers, and streams of several
documents.  See cyaml_parse(3).

Comments, from '#' to the end of the line.

A cyaml string, anchor or tag cannot contain a null byte, because each
of them is a null-terminated C string.  Encode such data, for example
as base64.  The parser does not decode a !!binary tag.
.fi
.SH "ANCHORS AND ALIASES"
.nf
"&name" on a node makes that node an anchor.  This is also true for a
mapping key.  Later in the same document, "*name" refers to that node.
The parser replaces each alias with an independent deep copy of the
anchored node.  Therefore, the tree has no shared nodes.  A change to the
copy has no effect on the original, and a change to the original has
no effect on the copy.  An anchor applies only in its own document of
a stream.  A node can have at most one anchor and one tag, in either
sequence.  An alias can have no anchor and no tag.

Each copy that an alias or a merge key makes counts against a fixed
expansion limit.  Therefore, a small document cannot expand into a tree of
unlimited size.  cyaml_parse(3) gives the numbers.
.fi
.SH "MERGE KEYS"
.nf
A mapping entry with the key "<<" merges other mappings into its own
mapping.  The key is a merge key in two cases:
  - It is a plain scalar with no tag.
  - It has the core merge tag, plain or quoted.  The core merge tag is
    !!merge, a %TAG shorthand of it, or the verbatim
    !<tag:yaml.org,2002:merge>.
A "<<" that is quoted and has no tag is an ordinary key.  A "<<" with
a different tag, such as !!str, is also an ordinary key.  This rule
applies in all notations:
  - block mappings and flow mappings
  - implicit keys and explicit keys
  - the one-entry mapping of a "- <<: value" or "[<<: value]" sequence
    entry.

The value of a merge key is one mapping, or a sequence of mappings.
Usually these are aliases.  A different value is a parse error.

The parser copies the members of each source into the mapping.  Then
it removes the "<<" entry.  The precedence rules are:

  - A key that the mapping defines itself always keeps its own value.
    This is true before and after the "<<" entry.
  - For a sequence of sources, an earlier source has priority over a
    later source.

The merged members go in the place of the "<<" entry in the member
order of the mapping.  They come after the explicit members before
"<<", and before the explicit members after "<<".  The parser adds
them source by source, and each source in its own member order.  For
example:

    defaults: &d {timeout: 30, retries: 3}
    service:
      <<: *d
      timeout: 60

gives service the members retries: 3 and then timeout: 60.
.fi
.SH "DUPLICATE KEYS"
.nf
The parser accepts a key that occurs more than one time in a mapping.
The last value is the value of the key.  The key keeps the place of its
first occurrence.  The parser frees the earlier value, and it does not
report this.  Common YAML parsers do the same, but YAML 1.2 does not
require it.  Therefore, a program must not expect that a YAML parser finds
a duplicate key.
.fi
.SH "OWNERSHIP"
.nf
cyaml_parse(3), cyaml_clone(3) and the cyaml_create_*(3) functions
give a tree that the caller owns.  The caller frees it with
cyaml_destroy(3).  cyaml_get(3), cyaml_list_get(3),
cyaml_dictionary_get(3) and the dictionary cursor give borrowed
references.  The tree keeps the ownership of these nodes.
cyaml_list_push(3) and cyaml_dictionary_set(3) take the child.  Their
pages give the one return code that leaves the child with the caller.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
A tree is not thread safe.  Several threads can read one tree at the
same time while no thread changes it.  Separate trees are independent.
Separate threads can parse and serialize at the same time.
.fi
